      <h3>MCQ Stability: The Most Consistent Element</h3>
      <p><span class="pill pill-mcq">MCQs</span> represent the single most stable element of the examination across all years. Every paper from 2015 to 2025 contains exactly <strong>15 MCQs</strong>, with no exceptions. This rigidity reflects a deliberate policy decision by BIEK to maintain a fixed objective assessment component.</p>
      <p>MCQs cover all major topic areas proportionally: database concepts, C language syntax, operators, control structures, and MS-Access. Their questions are typically definition-recall or identification-type, testing breadth of knowledge.</p>

      <h3>Short Questions: The Primary Variable</h3>
      <p><span class="pill pill-short">Short Questions</span> are the only element that has changed over time. The count jumped from <strong>9 (2015–2016)</strong> to <strong>18 (2017–2019)</strong> and then settled at <strong>15 (2021–2025)</strong>. This suggests the board experimented with a heavier short-answer load and then moderated to a middle ground.</p>
      <p>The current 15-question format provides better topic coverage than the original 9, while being less exhaustive than the expanded 18-question format.</p>

      <h3>Long Questions: Fixed at 3 Per Year</h3>
      <p><span class="pill pill-long">Long Questions</span> are the most stable in absolute count (always 3) but the most variable in content structure. Each contains 2–3 sub-parts (a, b, c), effectively meaning each "Long Question" covers 6–9 distinct topic assessments. The consistent count of 3 long questions masks the substantive depth they test.</p>

      <div class="insight-box">
        <strong>Type Composition Insight:</strong> In the current (2021–2025) format, MCQs make up <strong>45.5%</strong>, Short Questions <strong>45.5%</strong>, and Long Questions <strong>9.1%</strong> of the total question count. This near-equal split between MCQs and Short Questions means students must equally master recall-based and explanation-based answering.
