  <div class="section">
    <div class="section-header">
      <div class="section-number">6</div>
      <h2>Conclusions &amp; Limitations</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="section-body">
      <h3>Key Conclusions</h3>
      <ol>
        <li><strong>The exam has not remained fully stable — meaningful structural shifts occurred twice.</strong> The expansion in 2017 (to 36 questions) and the moderation in 2021 (to 33 questions) represent deliberate policy changes. The current 33-question format has now been in place for five consecutive years and is the de facto current standard.</li>
        <li><strong>MCQs (15/year) and Long Questions (3/year) are permanently fixed constants.</strong> Only the Short Questions section has ever changed. Curriculum planners can treat MCQs and Long Questions as structurally fixed.</li>
        <li><strong>Database and C Programming are co-equal pillars.</strong> Neither dominates; both appear across all question types in all years. A student who masters only C programming or only database concepts will underperform.</li>
        <li><strong>The curriculum is gradually modernising.</strong> Topics like arrays, pointers, normalization, ER diagrams, ACID properties, and binary search are emerging post-2021. Educators using only pre-2021 question banks for preparation risk leaving students underprepared.</li>
        <li><strong>Loop and operator concepts are the single most consistently tested area.</strong> They appear in all 10 years, across all three question types. Any exam preparation strategy must treat these as non-negotiable core content.</li>
        <li><strong>The Phase 2 expansion (2017–2019) may have included both annual and supplementary exam questions.</strong> The dataset documentation notes that some years may include both annual and supplementary papers, which could partially explain the jump to 18 Short Questions in those years.</li>
      </ol>

      <h3>Data Limitations</h3>
      <div class="alert-box">
        <ul>
          <li><strong>2020 is missing entirely.</strong> The dataset contains no questions from 2020. This gap prevents continuous trend analysis across all 11 years and may affect year-over-year calculations presented in this report.</li>
          <li><strong>Annual vs. Supplementary papers are not distinguished.</strong> The dataset does not encode whether a question came from the annual or supplementary (supplementary) examination. Years with higher question counts (2017–2019) may reflect combined paper data.</li>
          <li><strong>Community-compiled dataset.</strong> This is not an officially published BIEK database. Minor transcription errors may exist in some questions. The <code>$</code> delimiter is non-standard and may introduce parsing artefacts in keyword analysis.</li>
          <li><strong>Topic matching is keyword-based.</strong> The topic frequency analysis used keyword matching, not expert pedagogical classification. Some questions may be undercounted or overcounted in topic categories due to keyword ambiguity.</li>
          <li><strong>327 questions is a compact dataset.</strong> While sufficient for structural analysis, fine-grained year-by-year topic trend detection has limited statistical power. Patterns identified here are directional, not conclusive.</li>
          <li><strong>Question text quality varies.</strong> Some questions appear truncated or contain OCR artefacts from scan-to-text conversion, particularly in mathematical expression fields.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <h3>Recommendations for Future Analysis</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>Source and integrate 2020 paper data to fill the gap and enable full 11-year continuity.</li>
        <li>Tag each row with <code>annual</code> vs. <code>supplementary</code> to enable separate trend analysis for each examination sitting.</li>
        <li>Introduce a structured topic taxonomy for expert classification to supplement keyword-based analysis.</li>
        <li>Extend this dataset beyond 2025 to track whether the emerging topic set (normalization, pointers, ACID) solidifies into recurring themes.</li>
      </ul>
